VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-a11y
FAMILY: anthropic
TASK: S4
ATTEMPT: 2
---
METHOD: Real browser (Playwright/chromium, reused install from prior session),
not template reading. Built the worktree (go build ./cmd/server) and ran it
against an isolated data dir in scratchpad (BUDGET_DATA_DIR override; repo
data/ and the working tree were never touched, confirmed via `git status`
before/after). Created two accounts (a-wide: pattern "usaa", b-narrow:
pattern "usaa-checking*.csv") plus real *.csv files on disk via the actual
"Add an account" form to force accounts.OverlapWarnings non-empty through
the same code path a user hits. Drove every check below with real DOM
queries, MutationObserver, node-identity checks, keyboard-only interaction
(page.keyboard.press, never .click for the dismiss control), computed-style
contrast math cross-checked with axe-core 4.13 (both a Node-embedded run and
`npx @axe-core/cli`), and a javaScriptEnabled:false browser context. Server
killed after testing; `git status` confirms nothing in the repo was written
except this verdict file.

POINT 10, live-region existence/persistence — PASS.
#accounts-warnings-region is present at GET /accounts, outside #accounts-list
(`list.contains(region) === false`), with role="status" aria-live="polite"
aria-atomic="true" class="sr-only". Tagged the node with a probe attribute
and held a JS reference before two separate HTMX swaps (an unrelated edit,
then a pattern edit); after both, `region === window.__region` and the probe
attribute survived — this is the same physical node across swaps, unlike
attempt 1 where the node was destroyed and recreated. The visible
#accounts-warnings-banner carries no role/aria-live (confirmed via
getAttribute), so exactly one thing announces.

SAME-TEXT GUARD, both halves — PASS.
With a MutationObserver on the region: (a) an unrelated mutation (editing
b-narrow's low_balance_threshold, warnings text unchanged) produced
mutationCount=0 — the guard correctly did not touch the DOM. (b) A real
content change (editing b-narrow's patterns to also overlap usaa-credit.csv)
produced mutationCount=1 and the region's text grew to include the second
warning clause. The guard neither withholds real updates nor rewrites
unchanged text.

POINT 10, focus after HTMX swap replacing the focused element — OBSERVATION,
unchanged, not charged to S4. Focused b-narrow's "Save changes" button,
edited its threshold (unrelated to the "Add an account" form), submitted;
document.activeElement after the swap was #acct-new-id. Grepped
accounts.html: the unconditional `data-focus-target="id"` on that field
(line ~280) is untouched by this diff (git diff scoped to handlers.go,
handlers_test.go, and the warnings-specific HTML/script edits). This is the
same bug attempt 1 recorded as pre-existing and out-of-scope; attempt 2 did
not fix it and did not make it worse.

POINT 14, dismiss control — PASS.
Real `<button type="button" data-dismiss-warnings aria-label="Dismiss
pattern overlap warning">`, tabIndex 0. Reached it with keyboard-only Tab
navigation from document start (10 tabs, no click anywhere), confirmed a
visible focus indicator (computed outline + a 2px amber box-shadow ring
appears only while focused), and activated it with Enter (not .click()) —
the banner disappeared. sessionStorage (not localStorage) holds the
dismissal keyed on WarningsKey. Verified: dismissal survives a same-session
reload (banner absent, sessionStorage key intact); editing b-narrow's
patterns to add a second overlapping file changes WarningsKey and the
banner reappears even though the old key is still stored (key comparison
correctly fails); a brand-new browser context (fresh session storage)
shows the banner again with no stored key, i.e. dismissal does not persist
beyond the session; and throughout, #accounts-warnings-data /
#accounts-warnings-region kept the warning text populated even while
dismissed — dismissal is purely a visual/client-side suppression, it does
not suppress the server-side computation.

POINT 10 (second clause) — FAIL. "destructive or state-changing actions ...
announce their result via an aria-live="polite" region," and focus handling
around the dismiss action.
Drove the dismiss button by keyboard only and instrumented
document.activeElement plus every [aria-live] node before/after. Result:
immediately after Enter, document.activeElement is <body> (the button's
container, which held focus, was removed via banner.remove()) — no focus
management moves it anywhere inside the page. The next Tab press lands on
#acct-new-id, i.e. the tab sequence effectively restarts near the top of
the document rather than continuing from where the banner was — a keyboard
user loses their place. Simultaneously, #accounts-warnings-region's content
is left completely unchanged by the dismissal (it still holds the old
warning text, exactly as before), and no other aria-live node reports
anything about the dismissal happening. So a screen-reader user who
activates "Dismiss pattern overlap warning" gets no confirmation that
anything happened and loses their keyboard position — this is precisely the
failure mode the task brief asked to be ruled on ("a keyboard user who
dismisses a banner must not be dumped at the top of the document with no
announcement"), and it reproduces. Dismissing is a state-changing action
(visible -> hidden) not gated to "confirm/reject, delete" by the
constitution's wording ("destructive OR state-changing"), so point 10's
second clause applies and is not met. This is new surface area introduced
by S4 (the dismiss button did not exist in attempt 1), not a pre-existing
bug, and is in scope.

POINTS 7/12, contrast — PASS, arithmetic independently reproduced, no new
failures anywhere.
Measured actual getComputedStyle colors, composited them against their real
ancestor background stack (not assumed swatch values), and computed WCAG
relative-luminance contrast myself, then cross-checked with axe-core's
color-contrast rule scoped directly to #accounts-warnings-banner:
  LIGHT: explanatory text-xs (rgb(180,83,9) "amber-700" on composited
    rgb(255,251,235)) = 4.842:1 — matches the worker's claimed figure
    exactly. Dismiss-icon color is also amber-700 (not amber-500) = 4.842:1.
    axe scoped scan: 0 color-contrast violations.
  DARK: explanatory text-xs (amber-400 on composited ~rgb(84,67,65)) =
    5.587:1; dismiss-icon (amber-400, same bg) = 5.587:1; heading text
    (amber-300) = 6.468:1. axe scoped scan: 0 violations.
Full-page axe-core scans (programmatic axe.run with wcag2a/wcag2aa/
wcag22aa/best-practice tags, AND a separate `npx @axe-core/cli` run) in all
four combinations — light/with-warning, dark/with-warning, light/
no-warning (edited b-narrow to a disjoint pattern, confirmed banner and
region both go empty), dark/no-warning — show exactly one violation type in
every case: color-contrast on the two pre-existing "Delete account" button
nodes (form[hx-post=".../delete"] > .bg-gray-200...), identical targets to
attempt 1's documented baseline. No new violation of any kind (contrast,
name, landmark, heading-order, region) in any theme/state combination.

STRUCTURE (points 1-3, 9) — PASS, undisturbed.
Exactly one <h1> ("Accounts"); heading order h1 -> h2 ("Add an account",
"Your accounts", per-account h3, "Unassigned files") -> h3 -> h4 ("Matched
files", "Balance anchors"), no skipped levels. Exactly one <main>, one
<nav>, one <footer>. Matches attempt-1's baseline; the diff only touched
the warnings block and script, per `git diff --stat`.

JAVASCRIPT DISABLED — RULING: acceptable, matches the task's stated bar.
Loaded /accounts in a `javaScriptEnabled: false` browser context and
inspected the raw served HTML. The visible amber banner text ("Pattern
overlap warning" + the list items) is present as ordinary static markup —
it does not vanish, and a screen-reader or sighted user encountering it in
normal document order still gets the information without any script
running. #accounts-warnings-region is also server-rendered with the
warning text already inside it (role="status" aria-live="polite"), so nothing
is missing from the DOM; whether a given screen reader treats content
already present at initial load as an "announcement" vs. just readable text
is a known cross-AT inconsistency outside this task's control, and either
way the information reaches the user. The dismiss button is present in the
DOM but inert (its click handler never attaches without JS) — degraded
dismissal, which the task brief accepts. No regression: nothing vanishes.

ESCAPING — PASS.
Registered a real file on disk with a name containing a double quote,
single quote, ampersand, and angle brackets (`usaa-que"test'&<b>.csv`),
forced it into an overlap warning, and fetched the RAW server response
(not Playwright's re-serialized DOM) to inspect the literal bytes.
data-warnings-text="...file &#34;usaa-que\&#34;test&#39;&amp;&lt;b&gt;.csv&#34;..."
and the #accounts-warnings-region text-node content use the same
`&#34;/&#39;/&amp;/&lt;/&gt;` entities — single-level encoding (no
double-escaping), correct for both the attribute-value context and the
text-node context. DOM-level check after parsing: region.childElementCount
== 0 (no injected elements), no stray <b> element from the filename,
script tag count unchanged. Go's html/template auto-escaping is doing its
job in both places S4 interpolates warning text.

OVERALL: FAIL. Every claim about the live-region redesign, the same-text
guard, the dismiss button's keyboard operability/session semantics, the
contrast numbers, and the escaping was independently reproduced and holds
(points 10-clause-1's persistence problem and point 14's total absence,
both of which failed attempt 1, are fixed). But the dismiss button itself
is new S4 surface area, and exercising it by keyboard surfaces a real,
reproducible point 10 (second clause) violation: dismissing the banner
announces nothing via any aria-live region and drops focus to <body>,
effectively resetting the keyboard user's position in the document. Fix
needed for attempt 3: after banner.remove(), move focus somewhere sensible
(e.g. the "Your accounts" heading, matching the existing htmx:afterSwap
fallback pattern already in this same script) and/or emit a short
confirmation via the existing #accounts-warnings-region or a second sr-only
live region ("Pattern overlap warning dismissed").
